{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01CwgHio45ApAJYXwrRsH2Fp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. I wrote **Q19: Scale RAG to 10M+ articles** (`answers/02-019-scale-rag-to-10m-articles-sharding-caching-retrieval-optimiz.md`). Here's what the note covers:\n\n**Three-pillar architecture:**\n1. **Index sharding** — partition 30–60M vectors across 10–15 shards (5M vectors each → <50ms ANN latency), scatter-gather with parallel fan-out, managed via Pinecone/Qdrant or self-hosted FAISS\n2. **Multi-layer caching** — embedding cache (Redis, SHA256 key), semantic similarity cache (GPTCache, cosine ≥ 0.97), response cache (Redis/CDN) — 20–40% of production queries are near-duplicates\n3. **Staged retrieval** — ANN top-100 (~40ms) → BM25/bi-encoder rerank top-20 (~15ms) → cross-encoder top-5 (~100ms, skipped for high-confidence ANN matches)\n\n**Bonus angles included:** CDC-triggered incremental ingestion (never full re-index), speculative shard timeout to bound p99 tail latency from slow shards, Pinecone vs self-hosted Qdrant cost comparison at scale (~$35/day vs ~$8/hr for 60M vectors), and per-source-type cache TTL strategy (news: 5min, legal: no response cache)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":335,"cache_read_input_tokens":60333,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":335,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e40343a-52be-4dd0-acdc-1df31cf8706d","uuid":"50513a0f-8db5-4d47-8cd2-528fd4b7a023"}
